      "writtenOn": "2022-01-25T16:02:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "0 is a valid IPv6 protocol, it\u0027s the hop-by-hop option. So it would be good to use something else such as -1 instead (see other comment about an UNSET constant). Otherwise, it\u0027s not possible to separate \"protocol 0\" from \"protocol is unset\".\n\nWhat we do here might depend on how the program works.\n1. If the BPF program uses 0 to mean \"no protocol\", then we might have to leave this as is for now. It\u0027s definitely going to be more common to say \"all protocols\" than to say \"protocol 0\".\n2. If the BPF program can enable/disable the protocol match independent of the protocol number, then we should use an UNSET constant here.\n\nIt looks like the BPF program already supports explicitly stating which parts of the packet must match (it\u0027s policy-\u003emask)",
